1
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:25,960
Picture a day like this. You sit down to solve
a coding interview problem and polish

2
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:31,060
your portfolio. Then you open a video with
this title: Developers are finished.

3
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:33,660
One side says you will fall behind without AI.

4
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:39,060
The other says using AI stops you from
learning. So should I turn it on or off?

5
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:43,340
I came here to study, and now I am questioning
my entire career.

6
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:46,780
You have not even started your first job, and
people are already declaring it extinct.

7
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:50,380
That is hard to laugh off. Everyone else seems
to be building things, while you are

8
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:53,900
still stuck setting up your environment. It
can feel like you alone are falling behind.

9
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:59,840
Today, I will do more than tell you not to
worry. Let us look at what helps AI-assisted

10
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:06,180
work become a skill you actually own, from
exams and interviews to your first job.

11
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:32,120
At this point, you might think: surely people
already working at companies

12
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:36,020
know what I should prepare for? Let us step
into an imaginary meeting room.

13
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:40,520
The boss says, Can AI do all of this? An
executive asks, Does that halve the cost?

14
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:44,360
The team lead concludes, Great, let us halve
the schedule too.

15
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:49,000
Nobody has reviewed the code, but the deadline
has already been optimized.

16
00:01:49,380 --> 00:01:52,680
That is a little exaggerated. But a senior job
title does not automatically

17
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:56,060
mean understanding a technology's capabilities
and limits.

18
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:58,960
Working once in a demo is not the same as
working reliably in our service.

19
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:04,260
That leaves job seekers frustrated.

20
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:08,860
You are expected to use AI well and have solid
fundamentals. So separate your goals.

21
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:11,840
Leave yourself time to think through what you
are learning, and get help with repetitive

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,580
work you already understand. Always follow the
tool-use rules for assignments and exams.

23
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:40,680
You paste in an error: Fix this. You try the
suggested code, and a different error appears.

24
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,840
Fix this too. Eventually, it runs. But you
cannot explain what changed.

25
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:51,040
When you are in a hurry, getting it to run is
a relief. But if the same problem

26
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:55,120
comes back tomorrow, you may have to start
that conversation all over again.

27
00:02:55,660 --> 00:03:00,880
Pick one error and investigate it. If a loop
should run five times but only runs four,

28
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:05,280
write down the expected result. Set a
breakpoint and follow the variables.

29
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:07,940
Find the point where reality stops matching
your expectations.

30
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:10,820
Fix the condition and check the same input
again. Read documentation or ask for a

31
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,680
hint if you get stuck. The important part is
not how many hours you struggled alone.

32
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:18,680
It is whether you can later explain the
mistake in one sentence.

33
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:43,300
Fundamentals matter. That is true. But the
list includes data structures,

34
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:47,240
algorithms, operating systems, networking, and
databases.

35
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:50,700
I came to prepare for a job, not enroll in
another degree.

36
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:54,320
You do not have to finish all of those
subjects before building anything.

37
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:57,860
Start with one question about your current
feature.

38
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,020
How should requests be handled in the order
they arrive? How should data be found by name?

39
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:07,040
A queue might address the first question, and
a dictionary the second.

40
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:09,780
Explore what changes as the data grows.
Fundamentals stop being

41
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:13,180
a memorization checklist and become reasons
for your decisions.

42
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:17,660
The same goes for networking and databases.
Trace where a request failed or why

43
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:21,580
the stored value differs, and learn the
concepts you need along the way. Instead of

44
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:27,280
mastering every subject at once, start with
one part of your project you cannot explain.

45
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:51,340
When you build a portfolio, it is tempting to
add one more technology.

46
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:57,560
Everyone else's project looks impressive.
Soon, your little notes app has

47
00:04:57,780 --> 00:05:00,540
several servers and a cache. You wanted a
notepad and nearly built a data center.

48
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:05,520
Now imagine an interviewer asks: Why did you
design it this way?

49
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:11,400
If your entire answer is, AI recommended it,
your own judgment is hard to see.

50
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:16,300
You do not need a grand explanation. I kept
the setup small because it is a single-user

51
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:19,080
program. I separated the processing because
concurrent edits were possible.

52
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:21,360
Start with specific reasons like those.

53
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:25,480
What matters is not using fewer technologies
for its own sake, but knowing why you

54
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:28,400
chose them. Trace the path from input to
processing to output in your own words.

55
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:33,040
Wherever your explanation stalls is a good
place to study next.

56
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:58,920
You definitely built it at home. It ran, and
reading the code made sense.

57
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:03,680
Then an exam or interview asks you to
implement it without help.

58
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:07,960
On paper or in an editor, suddenly you cannot
even write the first line.

59
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:12,240
You can picture the finished screen, but your
hands stop at the function name.

60
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:17,220
Nerves and time pressure matter too. Getting
stuck once does not mean you have no ability.

61
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:22,220
But recognizing an answer and planning the
steps from scratch need separate practice.

62
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:26,920
Suppose you must remove duplicate names while
keeping the order of their first appearance.

63
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:30,940
Before writing code, write a small input and
the output you want.

64
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:35,480
Decide where to remember names you have seen
and where to put new ones.

65
00:06:36,060 --> 00:06:39,060
Then turn those plain-language steps into
loops and conditions. If you get stuck,

66
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:43,820
ask AI for just the next condition to check,
not the entire solution.

67
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:47,800
Once you solve it, close the answer and try a
small variation with different input.

68
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:52,760
Rather than memorizing one problem, practice
how to start from a blank page.

69
00:07:13,140 --> 00:07:16,740
If you want to build something yourself, a
small game like Tetris is a good option.

70
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:21,320
Just drop a few blocks, right? That is an easy
assumption to make.

71
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:24,900
But while the blocks fall, your to-do list
keeps stacking up.

72
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:29,760
Store the board in a two-dimensional array and
represent a four-cell piece with coordinates.

73
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:34,460
It moves down over time, and key presses move
it sideways or rotate it.

74
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:38,200
You also have to check whether the next
position overlaps a wall or another block.

75
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:43,220
When it cannot move farther down, lock it into
the board.

76
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:45,620
Remove completed rows and move the rows above
them down. Decide what happens when

77
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:49,220
rotation is blocked near a wall, and when the
game ends if a new piece cannot spawn.

78
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:51,700
If you do not define the rules, your bugs will
do it for you.

79
00:07:52,260 --> 00:07:56,500
Building Tetris does not guarantee you a job.
It is practice connecting input,

80
00:07:56,500 --> 00:08:00,640
data, collision checks, and state changes into
something finished.

81
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:03,740
Before adding more features to a huge
unfinished project,

82
00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:07,660
complete something small that you can explain
yourself.

83
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:30,660
It works now. You show a friend, and they
immediately shove a piece against

84
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:36,180
the wall and hammer the rotate button. Why
would you rotate it there?

85
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:39,940
Well, my program is the one that lets them
press that button.

86
00:08:40,280 --> 00:08:43,960
If you have only dropped one piece in the
middle, try other conditions.

87
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:48,400
What if two rows fill at once? What if a piece
rotates next to stacked blocks?

88
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:52,740
What if there is nowhere for a new piece to
spawn?

89
00:08:52,740 --> 00:08:55,860
What if keys are pressed after game over? The
rules must hold outside your favorite demo.

90
00:08:55,860 --> 00:09:01,220
Write the expected result and compare it with
what happens. Reject rotations through a wall.

91
00:09:01,220 --> 00:09:06,240
Clear only the completed rows, together. Keep
problem inputs as tests,

92
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:10,020
so you can see whether a later change breaks
the same behavior again.

93
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:13,940
Passing tests does not mean every problem is
gone.

94
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:18,720
But you can add specific checked conditions to
the claim that it works.

95
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:22,520
You can also ask AI to suggest edge cases you
may have missed.

96
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:47,600
Job seekers face another practical problem.
You already pay for courses and

97
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:52,260
living expenses. If every useful tool needs
another payment, that adds up.

98
00:09:52,540 --> 00:09:56,120
Your developer-tool subscriptions start work
before your salary does.

99
00:09:56,380 --> 00:10:00,500
Models such as Astra and Claude Fable also
require attention to usage costs.

100
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:04,180
The on-screen prices are standard API usage
rates checked in September 2026.

101
00:10:04,180 --> 00:10:07,720
They are not monthly subscription fees.

102
00:10:07,791 --> 00:10:12,300
Input and output are billed separately using
units of text called tokens.

103
00:10:12,300 --> 00:10:19,400
Your actual bill depends on usage, retries,
and discounts for reusing the same content.

104
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:23,780
Even a higher unit price can mean lower cost
per task if fewer attempts are needed.

105
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:27,820
Do not judge only by expensive or cheap. Look
at the whole job.

106
00:10:28,040 --> 00:10:33,920
Prices could rise, or free allowances could
shrink. That is not a prediction that they

107
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:37,780
definitely will. The point is to keep a way to
continue working if today's terms change.

108
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:43,060
Performance deserves the same approach. Test
supposedly faster code with the same input,

109
00:10:43,340 --> 00:10:46,280
and compare both time and results. This is not
advice to buy the most expensive tool.

110
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:50,080
Choose the help you need and expand what you
can verify yourself.

111
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:14,820
Now imagine you have started your first job.
Your feature works on screen.

112
00:11:15,180 --> 00:11:19,740
You tell your team lead, Almost done. But
empty input causes an error,

113
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:24,000
a failed save freezes the screen, and
integrating another feature breaks it again.

114
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:26,240
The next day, it is still almost done. The
feature moves,

115
00:11:26,440 --> 00:11:29,100
but the task is going nowhere. There was a gap
between building

116
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:34,240
the visible screen and finishing the work you
were assigned.

117
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:39,320
Delays are not always a junior developer's
fault. Requirements change,

118
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:43,260
schedules are tight, or nobody is available to
review the work.

119
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:47,980
Before struggling alone, agree on what counts
as done.

120
00:11:48,380 --> 00:11:52,240
Write down whether valid input alone is
enough, what message should appear on failure,

121
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:56,260
whether saved values are correct, and whether
integration works. Include the

122
00:11:56,640 --> 00:12:01,140
necessary checks for secrets, permissions, and
the suitability of new libraries.

123
00:12:01,420 --> 00:12:06,580
You can report progress differently too. Valid
input works; save-error handling is

124
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:10,840
still pending. I have checked these parts, and
I would like help reviewing this condition.

125
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:14,560
Put AI-generated code through the team's tests
and review process as well.

126
00:12:14,820 --> 00:12:16,960
This does not mean a junior developer carries
all the responsibility.

127
00:12:17,320 --> 00:12:21,340
Making progress and blockers visible helps
other people give you specific support.

128
00:12:41,780 --> 00:12:45,620
A senior developer says, I can build it from
scratch without AI. A job seeker might

129
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:52,000
think: You already have a job. Right now, I
just need to get my foot in the door.

130
00:12:52,180 --> 00:12:56,340
That is fair. Different starting points mean
we cannot demand that everyone spend their

131
00:12:56,440 --> 00:12:58,780
time the same way. The useful lesson is not a
senior developer's story of suffering. It is

132
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:04,200
the experience of starting from an empty file.
Age alone does not give you that experience.

133
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:09,480
You do not need to declare that you will build
everything yourself.

134
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:13,660
Choose just one feature: save a line of notes,
or move a Tetris piece one cell.

135
00:13:13,840 --> 00:13:17,820
Turn off AI autocomplete briefly and decide
for yourself where to begin.

136
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:22,100
If you prepare the runtime, required
libraries, and documentation in advance,

137
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:25,640
you can build and run local programs like
these without internet access.

138
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:29,860
Features that require an external server or a
new download are a separate matter.

139
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:33,800
Get help when you need it. Then close the
answer and start again. Compare your own

140
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:40,580
version with the AI-assisted version, and
record what you learned and fixed.

141
00:13:40,860 --> 00:13:45,760
Move from being unable to work without the
tool to being able to begin, even slowly.

142
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:09,540
Getting stuck in one exam, receiving a
disappointing application result,

143
00:14:09,540 --> 00:14:13,880
or delivering one task late does not erase
everything you have learned.

144
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:17,980
But hearing that things will be fine does not
instantly make the worry disappear either.

145
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:21,200
I cannot give you the answer to which tools
will appear or how hiring will change.

146
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:25,200
But if you can fix an error today that you
could not explain yesterday,

147
00:14:25,200 --> 00:14:28,940
that is a real change you keep.

148
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:34,040
Do not make today's plan too big. Take one
small input, process it,

149
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:38,540
check the result, and finish. Practice
explaining that

150
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:42,360
sequence in interviews and describing what
remains to your team.

151
00:14:42,740 --> 00:14:45,200
The goal is not to become someone who never
uses AI. It is to keep your

152
00:14:45,660 --> 00:14:49,620
own judgment when you use it, and still be
able to start when you cannot.

153
00:14:50,380 --> 00:14:53,680
Fundamentals and tool skills do not require
you to abandon one for the other.

154
00:14:54,100 --> 00:14:58,900
Set the AI hype aside for a moment and choose
one feature to finish with

155
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:02,240
your own hands today. Faster with AI, but not
helpless without it.

156
00:15:02,780 --> 00:15:06,480
Subscribe to tools if you want. Just do not
make your skills subscription-only.
